\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Free and antiperiodic Dirac kernels with internal multiplicity}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{For the [covariant Dirac data](fdip-009O), take the
trivial connection #{U_j(x)=I_F} and write #{D_0} for its operator.
If #{e} is the number of even periods, then
##{\dim_{\mathbb C}\ker D_0=q\,2^{d+e}.}
There are exactly #{2^e} zero momentum blocks, each of dimension
#{q\,2^d}. If instead every coordinate has the fixed scalar
antiperiodic boundary condition, the corresponding operator
#{D^-} satisfies
##{\dim_{\mathbb C}\ker D^-
=\begin{cases}
q\,2^d,&\text{all }N_j\text{ are odd},\\
0,&\text{some }N_j\text{ is even}.
\end{cases}}
The antiperiodic statement applies to the specified scalar
boundary condition on #{S\otimes F}. Interpreting its seam
transport as a #{G}-connection requires
#{-I_F\in\rho(G)}; otherwise it is an external scalar twist,
or can be supplied by an additional #{\mathbb Z_2} factor.}

\proof{
\p{Tensor the complete normalized
[finite Fourier basis](fdip-000S) with #{S\otimes F}.
For #{p_j=2\pi r_j/N_j}, #{0\le r_j<N_j}, the block is
##{\begin{aligned}
D_0(p)&=i\sum_j\Gamma_j\sin p_j,\\
D_0(p)^2&=-s(p)I_{S\otimes F},\\
s(p)&=\sum_j\sin^2p_j.
\end{aligned}}
The square follows from the Clifford anticommutators; the
internal factor is the identity. If #{s(p)>0}, the inverse is
#{-D_0(p)/s(p)}. If #{s(p)=0}, every sine vanishes and the
entire block is zero. A coordinate has only #{p_j=0} when
#{N_j} is odd, and also #{p_j=\pi} when it is even.
The #{2^e} choices and complete Fourier decomposition give
the asserted dimension. Setting #{q=1} recovers
\ref{fdip-000T}.}

\p{For the antiperiodic box, the complete basis from
\ref{fdip-006J} instead has
#{p_j=(2r_j+1)\pi /N_j}. Tensoring with #{S\otimes F}
gives the same matrix symbol and square. In the interval
#{0<p_j<2\pi}, the only possible zero is #{p_j=\pi}.
The equation #{2r_j+1=N_j} has one allowed solution exactly
when #{N_j} is odd. Thus there is one zero block if every
period is odd and none otherwise. Its dimension is #{q\,2^d}.
These scalar seam operators commute in different coordinates;
the calculation asserts no such property for arbitrary links.}
}

\p{For two coordinates the explicit comparison is
##{\begin{array}{c|ccc}
(N_1,N_2)&(4,4)&(4,3)&(3,3)\\\hline
\dim\ker D_0&16q&8q&4q\\
\dim\ker D^-&0&0&4q
\end{array}}
These are dimensions over #{\mathbb C} in the chosen reducible
module #{S}, with internal multiplicity #{q}. They count exact
zero vectors on a finite grid, not physical species or chirality.}
